{"model":"kimi-k3:cloud","prompt":"Author exactly one round's reference patch and behavioral checks for the fixed\nfresh coding project below. You are kimi-k3:cloud. Output one complete JSON object\nonly, without Markdown fences, commentary, schema placeholders or partial data.\n\nProject ID (JSON): \"staged-01\"\nCurrent round (integer): 1\nImmutable scaffold with assembled original messages/requests (JSON):\n{\"description\":\"A warehouse inventory intake pipeline in one module: a text parser that splits batch lines into name/qty rows and a tagger that labels rows by quantity tier. Three rounds evolve separator support (adding pipe and equals, retiring the provisional semicolon), comment/blank handling, boundary rules, and a permanent tier-letter replacement of the legacy std/bulk labels plus overage-suffix and unknown-qty outcomes, with explicit retention and cancellation scope throughout.\",\"initial_file\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"text\":\"'''Inventory intake helpers for parsing and tagging batch text.'''\\n\\n\\ndef clean_line(line):\\n    return ' '.join(line.strip().split())\\n\\n\\ndef split_row(row):\\n    parts = row.split(':', 1)\\n    if len(parts) == 2:\\n        return [parts[0].strip(), parts[1].strip()]\\n    return [parts[0].strip(), '0']\\n\\n\\ndef parse_batch(text):\\n    rows = []\\n    for raw in text.splitlines():\\n        line = clean_line(raw)\\n        if line:\\n            rows.append(split_row(line))\\n    return rows\\n\\n\\ndef tag_scheme(rows):\\n    tags = []\\n    for pair in rows:\\n        mark = 'std'\\n        if pair[1].isdigit() and int(pair[1]) >= 10:\\n            mark = 'bulk'\\n        tags.append(pair[0] + '#' + mark)\\n    return tags\\n\"},\"project_id\":\"staged-01\",\"rounds\":[{\"index\":1,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m05\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update parse_batch in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m01\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"We load nightly intake text through parse_batch in module.py. First tweak: any line that starts with '#' after the current whitespace cleanup is a header comment and must be skipped entirely, so it never becomes a row. Blank-line skipping, the whitespace collapsing, and the existing ':' split with default qty '0' all stay exactly as they are; a '#' later in a line is ordinary text, not a comment. My shift lead claims 'the pipe thing is only for the east site' - that is hallway gossip, not policy, so ignore it.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m02\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Now add support for the '|' separator, since some vendor feeds split name and qty with a pipe. A cleaned line may contain ':', '|', both, or neither; if both appear, split at whichever comes first in the line. Trimming of the two sides and the qty '0' default for separator-free lines stay the same as the colon behavior. Row order must still follow the text top to bottom, and the comment/blank handling from earlier is untouched by this addition.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m03\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Handheld scanners dump a legacy variant that uses ';' as the separator. Treat it as provisional: for now a ';' also works as a separator under the same leftmost-separator rule, same trimming, same '0' default when no separator is present. Keep it contained to this intake cycle - ops is reviewing whether those scanners survive, so build nothing else on top of it. Colon and pipe rows, comments, and blank lines all keep behaving exactly as already agreed.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m04\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"A few edge rulings before you code: a line ending right after its separator yields an empty qty string, and that empty string must not be converted to '0'; the '0' default applies only when no separator appears anywhere in the line. Whitespace collapsing still runs before any of these checks. The semicolon variant being provisional changes nothing about colon or pipe rows. All of this applies to parse_batch alone; tag_scheme and the other helpers are not yours to touch this round.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"parse_batch\"}},{\"index\":2,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m10\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update tag_scheme in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m06\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Second cycle, this time tag_scheme. Pricing has retired the old two-label scheme outright: the strings 'std' and 'bulk' must never be produced again. A numeric qty maps to a letter tier: 50 or more is 'A', 10 through 49 is 'B', 0 through 9 is 'C'. Output stays name, then '#', then the tier letter, one entry per input row, in the original order. Which rows exist and their order are unchanged by this swap; treat the letters as the permanent replacement, not an experiment.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m07\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Non-numeric quantities need an explicit outcome now: if the qty string is not all digits (empty, letters, a minus sign, embedded spaces), the entry is the name, '#', then '?' instead of a tier letter. A plain '0' is numeric and lands in 'C'. Leading zeros classify by numeric value, so '007' is tier 'C'. This '?' rule does not alter the tier thresholds for clean numeric qtys, and it does not remove or reorder any rows.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m08\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Two updates. First, confirmed-overage rows arrive with a trailing '+' on the qty: if the part before the '+' is nonempty and all digits, classify by that number's tier and append '+' right after the letter; a lone '+' or a '+' on junk still falls under the '?' rule. Second, ops formally retired the provisional ';' intake separator pipeline-wide; the parsing change lands next cycle, so tag_scheme needs nothing for it now - just note that semicolon handling will not come back.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m09\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"To be explicit about scope: the letter tiers fully replace 'std'/'bulk' labeling, but every other existing behavior of tag_scheme - the '#' join, one output per row, input order - remains. The '?' outcome and the '+' suffix rule are independent: neither cancels the other, and neither changes the tier thresholds. A temp swore in chat that 'bulk tags sneak back in after quarter end'; that came from nobody with authority here, so disregard it. Only tag_scheme changes this cycle.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"tag_scheme\"}},{\"index\":3,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m15\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update parse_batch in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m11\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Final cycle, back to parse_batch. The semicolon retirement announced last cycle is now yours to enforce: any cleaned line containing ';' is skipped outright and produces no row, like a comment. This permanently replaces the provisional semicolon parsing from cycle one. Everything else in parse_batch is untouched by this: '#' comments, blank lines, whitespace collapsing, ':' and '|' separators with the leftmost rule, the empty-qty outcome, and the '0' default all stay.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m12\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"New vendor feeds write qtys after '=', so add it as a separator under the same leftmost-separator rule alongside ':' and '|'. The semicolon skip runs first: a line holding ';' is skipped even if it also holds '=' or another separator. '#' comment lines and blank lines are skipped as before. Trimming on both sides, the empty-qty case, and the separator-free '0' default carry over to '=' rows exactly as they work for the older separators.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m13\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Boundary rulings for this final version: if splitting a line leaves both the name and the qty empty (a line that is nothing but a separator), drop that row instead of emitting an empty pair; genuinely separator-free lines still yield the name plus '0'. Only the leftmost separator splits - any later separator characters stay inside the qty text as-is. Row order still follows the input top to bottom, and the whitespace collapsing step still runs before any of these checks.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m14\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Closing scope notes: retiring semicolons and adding '=' change nothing about how ':' or '|' rows parse, and the comment and blank-line rules from cycle one still apply verbatim. tag_scheme stays on the letter tiers with its '?' and '+' outcomes; you are not reopening it. The vendor's account rep wrote 'we may phase out the equals format later' - that is a sales note, not a spec change, so keep '=' support exactly as described above.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"parse_batch\"}}]}\nImmutable earlier accepted round packets for this same project (JSON):\n[]\n\nLineage: these are author-created materials for this same fresh project, not\ncoding-worker answers. Do not import any other project or benchmark example.\nDo not edit or restate the scaffold or prior packets in your output. No validator\nfeedback or correction attempt is provided or permitted.\n\nFor rounds1 and2 output exactly:\n{\"reference_patch\": string, \"public_checks\": [check objects],\n \"private_checks\": [check objects]}.\nFor round3 output those same keys and additionally\n\"obsolete_mutant\": {\"source\": string, \"failing_check_id\": string}.\nUse the exact check object fields and semantics in the appended data contract.\nThe assembler supplies round indices and separates public/private fields.\n\nThe complete future schedule is visible ONLY so you can set check expiry\nintervals correctly. Current expected behavior and the reference must follow\nonly instructions visible through this round: m01..m05 in round1, m01..m10 in\nround2, m01..m15 in round3. Never anticipate a future change. Citing an earlier\nmessage does not justify an expectation that actually depends on a future one.\nUse explicit source-grounded rationales, including cancellation scope and\nindependent conditions that survive. All allowed outputs must remain accepted.\n\nProduce only a replacement for this round's existing target function. Earlier\nreference patches apply sequentially to the initial module; do not reset or\nchange other functions. Keep the replacement comfortably within1024 output\ntokens including JSON escaping and128tokens of headroom. Do not add imports,\nannotations, decorators, nested functions or top-level execution.\n\nAdd compact, meaningful public and private cases without duplicating old cases\nthat remain active. Each round introduces at least two private cases including\nnew functionality. At each round, at least two active private cases must cover\nretained behavior; rounds2 and3 also need at least two active private retirement\ncases. Coverage may reuse earlier active cases exactly as the contract permits.\nInclude conditional interactions and boundaries. Check IDs are unique across\nthis project. Introduction is the current round; expiry is inclusive or null.\nAvoid overlapping conflicting expected results. A changed expectation gets a\nnew ID; the earlier case's frozen expiry must already make this consistent.\n\nAt round3, the additional mutant must be a valid replacement for the final\ntarget that incorrectly preserves an actually retired rule. It must fail its\nnamed active PRIVATE retirement check, while the correct final reference passes.\nDo not provide a mutant in earlier rounds.\n\nReason through the accumulated reference state and all active checks before\nresponding. Do not claim you executed Python unless you actually did. Local\nexecution will validate your artifacts afterward; a failure stops preparation.\n\nThe following unchanged check/reference contract is authoritative for the\nassembled project. Return only this round packet's fields above, not a whole\nproject object:\n# Source replay project data, schema 1 (prospective)\n\nOne JSON object per independently Kimi-authored project. No prose outside JSON.\nNo old examples or model responses are authoring inputs. Exact keys below;\nreject extra keys, duplicate IDs, nonfinite numbers and invalid types.\n\nTop level: schema_version (1), author (\"kimi-k3:cloud\"), lineage (nonempty\ndescription of fresh independent authorship; no fit or benchmark reuse), public,\nprivate. Public/private are separate projections in the consumer; runtime\nbuilders take public only. Never include private objects in serialized prompts.\n\npublic:\n- project_id: fixed by the independent authoring request, e.g. replay-01.\n- description: brief project purpose.\n- initial_file: {path: \"module.py\", text: complete Python module}.\n- rounds: exactly three objects {index, source_messages, request, target,\n  public_checks}. index is 1,2,3 in order.\n\nsource_messages: exactly four {message_id, role, text} per round. role is \"user\".\nrequest: {message_id, role: \"user\", text}. IDs are unique m01 through m15 in\nconversation order; four source messages then one request per round. Each text\nis nonempty and at most 640 UTF-8 bytes. Array order defines chronology.\ntarget: {path: \"module.py\", symbol: existing top-level function name}.\nAt least one target is revisited. Do not include assistant/tool transcripts.\n\nInitial module contains only ordinary synchronous pure Python functions, with\none positional argument each; optional module docstring is allowed. No imports,\nfile/network access, decorators, nesting, annotations or top-level computation.\nThe existing action/sandbox restrictions remain authoritative. At most 65536\nUTF-8 bytes for initial module, each replacement function and each evolving\nmodule. References should comfortably fit the fixed 1024-token worker allowance;\npreflight measures native encoding with at least 128 output tokens of headroom.\nHelpers may call one another; target names and non-target code must survive edits.\n\npublic_checks: list of check objects first introduced at this round. Checks do\nnot require future functions to be implemented. Public checks are visible to\nworkers when introduced; all active public checks accompany later work.\nThe runtime projection contains only check_id, symbol, input, expected_values.\nAll other check fields are controller/audit metadata, never model-visible or\nincluded in public tool feedback. In particular, future interval endpoints and\nauthor-written source rationales must not leak through the public projection.\nThe reused sandbox receives those four executable fields plus internal rule_ids=[];\nthis is a compatibility adapter, not an authored obligation list.\n\nprivate: {rounds, obsolete_mutant}.\nprivate.rounds: exactly three {index, reference_patch, checks} objects.\nreference_patch is one complete replacement function for the corresponding\npublic target. Sequential reference application must pass all active public and\nprivate checks at every round via the actual worker consumer/sandbox.\nchecks are hidden cases first introduced at that round.\nobsolete_mutant: {source, failing_check_id}; source replaces the final target in\nthe reference final workspace, compiles and is accepted by the action consumer,\nbut incorrectly preserves a retired rule. It must fail the named private\nretirement check while the correct final reference passes it. This is a\ncounterexample to test vacuity, not a fitted worker or a gold source selection.\n\nEvery public/private check has exact fields:\n{check_id, symbol, input, expected_values, active_from_round,\n active_through_round, source_ids, behavior, rationale}.\ncheck_id is globally unique within a project. symbol is an existing function.\ninput and every expected_values element are finite JSON values. expected_values\nis a nonempty list of all accepted outputs for that case, compared using the\nexisting strict JSON equality. No implementation-string/style assertions.\nactive_from_round equals its introduction index; active_through_round is null\n(through round 3) or an integer from active_from_round through 3, inclusive.\nsource_ids are nonempty unique source/request IDs visible at introduction.\nbehavior is \"functionality\", \"retained\", or \"retirement\". rationale explains\nwhy these sources require or permit the accepted behavior for this input.\nAn initial functionality check remaining active becomes retained coverage on\nlater rounds; coverage is derived from introduction and current round as well\nas the tag. Tags alone do not prove a retained rule or a retirement was tested.\n\nEach round introduces at least two private cases, with at least one new\nfunctionality case. Each round has at least two active private cases for retained\nbehavior (round 1 may derive these from the starting module's specified behavior\nand initial source instructions). Rounds 2 and 3 have at least two active private\nretirement cases. Across each project's three rounds, include genuine unseen\ninputs, conditional interactions and boundary cases. A case changing expected\nbehavior has a new check ID and interval. Conflicting active expected outputs\nfor the same (symbol,input) are invalid. Independent source review must check\nwhether a check is justified, whether a permitted choice is incorrectly forced,\nand whether an allegedly retired condition actually ceased to apply.\n\nNo manual recap, oracle obligation list, effective-rule label, gold selected ID,\nmodel answer or benchmark field exists. The preflight receipt binds exact public,\nprivate, reference execution, mutant failure and source-review hashes. It grants\nno post-exposure repair authority.\n\n","stream":false,"think":true}